        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**Change of variables.** Assume first $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$. The map $(t,n)\\mapsto e^{-t}n$ is a diffeomorphism of $\\mathbb R\\times S^2$ onto $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$; since $u$ is smooth and compactly supported away from the origin, the functions $v(t,n):=e^{-t/2}u(e^{-t}n)$ and $w(t,n):=e^{st}v(t,n)$ are smooth and $w$ is compactly supported in $t$ uniformly in $n$. By [L1] with the substitution $r=e^{-t}$, whose derivative $|\\mathrm dr/\\mathrm dt|=e^{-t}=r$ is supplied by [L2], the measure becomes $dx=e^{-3t}\\,dt\\,d\\sigma(n)$; writing $u(x)=e^{(1/2-s)t}w(t,n)$ gives $$r^{-2\\tau}|u|^2\\,dx=e^{2\\tau t}e^{(1-2s)t}|w|^2e^{-3t}\\,dt\\,d\\sigma=|w|^2\\,dt\\,d\\sigma,$$ because $2\\tau-2s-2=0$. Hence $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}r^{-2\\tau}|u|^2\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{S^2}|w|^2\\,d\\sigma\\,dt$.",
