          "claim": "**Spherical Laplacian and the polar form of $\\Delta$.** For $g\\in C^2(S^2)$ let $G(x):=g(x/|x|)$ be its degree-zero homogeneous extension to $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and define $\\Delta_{S^2}g(n):=\\Delta G(n)$ for $n\\in S^2$. This is well defined: by the chain rule, $\\Delta(G\\circ m_t)=t^2(\\Delta G)\\circ m_t$ for every $t>0$ and every $C^2$ function on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, while $G\\circ m_t=G$; hence $(\\Delta G)(tn)=t^{-2}\\Delta G(n)$, and $r^2\\Delta G(rn)=\\Delta G(n)$ does not depend on $r$, so the value at $r=1$ defines a function of $n$ alone. For $\\psi\\in C^2((0,\\infty))$ the product rule gives $\\nabla(\\psi(r)G(x))=\\psi'(r)G(x)\\,x/r+\\psi(r)\\nabla G(x)$, and $\\langle\\nabla G(x),x\\rangle/r=\\partial_rG=0$; using [L3] twice and the direct computation $\\operatorname{div}(x/r)=\\sum_i\\partial_i(x_i/r)=3/r-r^2/r^3=2/r$, $$\\operatorname{div}\\bigl(\\psi'G\\tfrac xr\\bigr)=\\psi''G+\\psi'G\\tfrac2r,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}(\\psi\\nabla G)=\\psi'\\langle\\tfrac xr,\\nabla G\\rangle+\\psi\\Delta G=\\psi\\,\\Delta G,$$ so that $\\Delta(\\psi(r)G)=\\bigl(\\psi''(r)+\\tfrac2r\\psi'(r)\\bigr)G+r^{-2}\\psi(r)\\Delta_{S^2}g(n)$, the last step using $\\Delta G(rn)=r^{-2}\\Delta_{S^2}g(n)$. In particular, for a harmonic homogeneous polynomial $H_l$ of degree $l$ and $h:=H_l|_{S^2}$, the identity with $\\psi(r)=r^l$ and $G(x)=H_l(x)/|x|^l$ gives $0=\\Delta H_l=r^{l-2}\\bigl(l^2+l+\\Delta_{S^2}h\\bigr)$, hence $\\Delta_{S^2}h=-l(l+1)h$. The same computation applied to a general $F\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$ with $f(r,n):=F(rn)$, whose gradient splits as $\\nabla F=(\\partial_rf)\\,n+r^{-1}\\nabla_{S^2,n}f$ into radial and tangential parts, gives the general polar form $\\Delta F(rn)=\\partial_r^2f+\\tfrac2r\\partial_rf+r^{-2}\\Delta_{S^2,n}f$; the divergence of a tangential field $Y$ with $Y(x)=\\tilde Y(x/|x|)$ is $r^{-1}\\operatorname{div}_{S^2}\\tilde Y$ in these coordinates, and $\\operatorname{div}_{S^2}\\nabla_{S^2}=\\Delta_{S^2}$.",
