        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-three-dimensional-radial-carleman-integer-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), in the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $s\\ge1$ be an integer and put $\\tau=s+1$. Write $r(x)=|x|$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and let $\\Delta$ be the Euclidean Laplacian ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). Then for every $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$, $$\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^3)}\\le\\frac{2}{s}\\,\\|r^{-\\tau+2}\\Delta u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^3)}.$$ Moreover, if $u\\in H^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ is compactly supported in an annulus $\\{0<a\\le|x|\\le b\\}$, then the weak second derivatives of $u$ exist as locally square integrable functions, $\\Delta u$ denotes their sum in the weak sense ([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]), and the same inequality holds for $u$. The weights $r^{-\\tau}$ and $r^{-\\tau+2}$ are bounded on every fixed compact annulus $\\{a_0\\le|x|\\le b_0\\}$ with $0<a_0\\le b_0$, and this boundedness on the support of the function is the only property of the weights used in the density extension below; the weight $r^{-\\tau}$ is unbounded at the origin for every $s\\ge1$, and the weight $r^{-\\tau+2}$ is unbounded at the origin for every $s\\ge2$ and equals the constant $1$ for $s=1$. That is why the extension is stated for functions whose support stays away from $r=0$. The constant $2/s$ is explicit and is not claimed to be sharp.",
