          "claim": "**Higher-order bootstrap and the classical boundary conclusion.** Suppose $U\\in H^{r+1}$ on the larger half-box for some $r\\ge1$; the data of the local system then satisfy $f\\in H^r$, each divergence datum $G_i\\in H^{r+1}$ and the lift-adjusted boundary datum $h$ is smooth because all coefficients are smooth. For a tangential multi-index $\\alpha$ of order $r$ put $Z:=D_y^\\alpha U$; differentiating the system of step 7.2 by $D_y^\\alpha$ keeps the principal matrix $a^{ij}$ and the Robin coefficient $\\beta$, and replaces the data by $f_\\alpha\\in L^2$, $G_\\alpha\\in H^1$ and $h_\\alpha\\in H^1(B)$ built from $D_y^\\alpha f$, $D_y^\\alpha G$ and the finitely many commutator terms $D_y^\\gamma(\\text{coefficient})\\,D_y^{\\alpha-\\gamma}U$ with $0<\\gamma\\le\\alpha$, all known by the induction hypothesis; the tangential traces of $Z$ are again zero in the first two components and free in the third, and localized tests for $Z$ are admissible by the test approximation of step 7.2; the differentiated Robin commutators contain traces of tangential derivatives of $U$ of order at most $r-1$, their tangential derivatives of one further order have $L^2$ traces because the corresponding interior derivatives of order at most $r$ belong to $H^1$; trace continuity and smooth approximation therefore place those commutators in $H^1(B)$ by [L10]. Applying step 8.1 to $Z$ gains two further tangential or mixed derivatives, and differentiating the normal-recovery equation of step 8.1 and inducting on the number of normal derivatives recovers the remaining ones; nested half-boxes and a finite boundary cover give $U\\in H^m$ on each smaller patch for every $m$, with constants depending on the data, $\\kappa$, the chart and the fixed nested covers. By the Sobolev embedding [L18] on a rounded bounded smooth subdomain of the chart that agrees with $s=0$ near the smaller patch and lies inside the larger half-box (hence an extension domain by [L13]) each $H^m$ clas