          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the weak-solvability and regularity lemmas below ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain in the local graph sense of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]], let $\\varepsilon\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega;\\operatorname{Sym}^3)$ be uniformly positive, that is $\\alpha|\\xi|^2\\le\\xi\\cdot\\varepsilon(x)\\xi\\le\\beta|\\xi|^2$ for all $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $x\\in\\Omega$ and some $0<\\alpha\\le\\beta<\\infty$, and let $\\rho$ be smooth on $\\overline\\Omega$. The Euclidean inner product is that of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; at a boundary point with outward unit normal $\\nu$ the outward conormal vector is $\\varepsilon\\nu$ and the outward conormal derivative is $\\nu\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u$. Here $H^1(\\Omega)$ and $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ are the Sobolev spaces of [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]] and the trace $T$ is that of the trace operator on the bounded $C^1$ domain $\\Omega$. Then:\n\n(a) **Dirichlet.** For every $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega)$ there is exactly one weak solution $u\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ with trace $Tu=g$ of $\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u\\,dx=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v\\,dx$ for all $v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)$; it depends continuously on the data, $\\|u\\|_{H^1}\\le C(\\|\\rho\\|_{L^2}+\\|g\\|_{H^{1/2}})$, has a $C^\\infty$ representative on $\\overline\\Omega$, and satisfies the classical equations $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=\\rho$ in $\\Omega$ and $u|_{\\partial\\Omega}=g$.\n\n(b) **Neumann.** Let $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega)$ be a smooth outward conormal datum satisfying $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\rho\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}g\\,dS=0$ on every connected component $\\Omega_j$ of $\\Omega$. Then there is a weak solution of $\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g\\,Tv$ for all $v\\in H^1(\\Omega)$; it is unique up to a separately chosen additive constant on each component, and imposing $\\int_{\\Omega_j}u=0$ on every component gives a unique normalised solution which is $C^\\infty$ on each closed component and satisfies the classical equations $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=\\rho$ in $\\Omega$ and $\\nu\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u|_{\\partial\\Omega}=g$. In particular on a connected domain the compatibility is $\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g=-\\int_\\Omega\\rho$, with a single additive constant.\n\n(c) **Transmission.** Let there be finitely many pairwise disjoint closed smooth interfaces strictly inside $\\Omega$, let $\\varepsilon$ be piecewise smooth on the closures of the resulting pieces and uniformly positive in the above sense, let $\\rho$ be piecewise smooth on the closed pieces and let $\\sigma$ be a smooth sheet charge on the union of the interfaces; the weak right-hand side is $\\int_\\Omega\\rho v+\\sum_\\Gamma\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma\\,Tv$, together with $\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g\\,Tv$ in the Neumann case. Prescribe either the smooth outer Dirichlet datum of (a), or the smooth outer Neumann datum of (b) with the componentwise compatibility condition $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\rho+\\sum_{\\Gamma\\subset\\Omega_j}\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}g=0$ for every component $\\Omega_j$. With outer Dirichlet data the globally $H^1$ solution is unique; with outer Neumann data it exists exactly when that compatibility holds and is then unique modulo componentwise additive constants, the zero componentwise means making it unique; the equal one-sided traces and $C^\\infty$ regularity on each closed side hold in either case, and the flux jump is $n\\cdot[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=-\\sigma$ in the sign convention of [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]], where $n$ is a chosen unit normal on the interface and $[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=\\varepsilon^+\\nabla u^+-\\varepsilon^-\\nabla u^-$ is the difference from the $n$-positive side to the other.\n\nEdge and junction points, reentrant corners, coefficient loss of regularity and general piecewise classical regularity beyond finitely many disjoint smooth interfaces are excluded. The existence content of all three parts is that of the two weak solvability lemmas; this theorem states the combined existence-and-regularity conclusions on the declared domains and adds no existence assertion beyond them.",
