          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-em-global-retarded-root",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $c>0$, $0\\le v_*<c$, and $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^1$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for all $s$. For every $(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ there is exactly one real root $s=s(x,t)$ of $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$, and $s\\le t$. Equality holds exactly when $x=z(t)$. Thus on the open off-instantaneous-worldline domain $D=\\{(x,t):x\\ne z(t)\\}$ one has $s<t$, $r=|x-z(s)|=c(t-s)>0$, and the root is $C^1$. With $n=(x-z(s))/r$, $\\beta=z'(s)/c$, and $\\kappa=1-n\\cdot\\beta$, its derivatives are $\\partial_t s=1/\\kappa$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$, where $1-v_*/c\\le\\kappa\\le1+v_*/c$. If $z$ is $C^k$, for an integer $k\\ge1$, the root is $C^k$ on $D$; in particular a $C^3$ trajectory gives a $C^3$ root. A smooth trajectory gives a smooth root. No higher regularity follows from a merely $C^1$ trajectory. These claims concern an all-time uniformly subluminal prescribed history; no finite-history or nonuniform-light-speed-limit assertion is made.",
