          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Proper-graph branch.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, let $z:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a $C^1$ trajectory, put $\\gamma(t):=(z(t),t)$ and let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open with $\\gamma[I]\\subseteq\\Omega$. Assume the graph map $\\gamma:I\\to\\Omega$ is **proper**: $\\gamma^{-1}[K]$ is a compact subset of $I$ for every compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$. For $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]) define\n\n$$\\langle\\rho,\\psi\\rangle:=q\\int_I\\psi(z(t),t)\\,dt,\\qquad \\langle J,\\psi\\rangle:=q\\int_I z'(t)\\,\\psi(z(t),t)\\,dt,$$\n\nthe second integral being vector-valued with components $\\langle J_i,\\psi\\rangle=q\\int_Iz_i'(t)\\psi(z(t),t)\\,dt$. Then $\\rho$ and $J$ are distributions on $\\Omega$ ([[def-distribution]]) and, with the distributional derivative of [[def-distributional-derivative]],\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\Omega).$$\n\nProperness is automatic if $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3\\times I$, and also if $I=\\mathbb R$.\n\n**Endpoint branch.** Suppose instead that $I=(a,b)$ is bounded with finite endpoints, that $z$ extends continuously to $[a,b]$, that the absolute improper integral $\\int_a^b\\lVert z'(t)\\rVert_2\\,dt$ is finite, and that both endpoint events $(z(a),a)$ and $(z(b),b)$ belong to $\\Omega$. Then the same formulas, with the integrals understood as improper integrals over $(a,b)$, define distributions $\\rho$ and $J$ on $\\Omega$, and\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=q\\,\\delta_{(z(a),a)}-q\\,\\delta_{(z(b),b)},$$\n\nthe Dirac distributions being those of [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]].\n\nMere graph containment $\\gamma[I]\\subseteq\\Omega$ does not make the pulled-back tests compactly supported, and it does not by itself give the source-free identity: in the endpoint branch the endpoint terms are present exactly as displayed, and they vanish on a test only when that test vanishes at the corresponding endpoint event. No Maxwell equation, no point self-force and no physical interpretation of $\\rho$ or $J$ is asserted; only the distributional identities for the prescribed support are proved.",
